# ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
# ║  PumpStriker — Environment Variables                          ║
# ║                                                               ║
# ║  1. Copy this file to .env                                    ║
# ║  2. Fill in the REQUIRED keys below                           ║
# ║  3. Run `npm install && npm run bot`                          ║
# ║                                                               ║
# ║  NEVER commit your real .env — it contains secrets.           ║
# ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
#
# Every key the bot reads is listed here, with its default as a commented line.
# Config parity is tested (src/config.test.ts): a key read by src/config.ts that is
# missing here, or a key here that nothing reads, fails the build.

# ── REQUIRED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your Telegram bot token from @BotFather (see README).
# Format: 123456789:AAH-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
TG_BOT_TOKEN=

# Your Telegram user_id (positive number). Get it from @userinfobot.
# The bot ONLY answers this user, and ONLY in your private chat with the bot
# (since 2026-09-29 it ignores groups, channels, edited messages and inline
# queries — a group id here no longer works).
TG_CHAT_ID=
# Wallet encryption secret (optional). Unset = derived from TG_BOT_TOKEN.
# Wallets are stored scrypt + AES-256-GCM (per-wallet salt). Older wallets are
# still readable — both WALLET_ENC_KEY and TG_BOT_TOKEN are tried — and are
# re-encrypted at startup (a wallets.json.bak-<time> backup is written first).
# UNSAFE (removed advice): never set this to the OLD bot token. Safe sequence before you rotate the
# bot token: while TG_BOT_TOKEN is still the old token, set this to a
# NEW random secret of at least 32 characters; start the bot once and check the log shows
# "[wallets] upgraded N wallet(s)" and no "could not be decrypted" warning; back up the private keys
# offline and move the wallets.json.bak-* files offline (they stay encrypted under the old token);
# only then rotate TG_BOT_TOKEN.
WALLET_ENC_KEY=

# Helius API key — sign up free at https://helius.dev
# Used for RPC calls, transaction sending, and balance reads.
HELIUS_KEY=

# Jupiter Developer Platform server key: REQUIRED for every trade that goes through Jupiter (every token
# that is not on a pump.fun bonding curve, and every trade the direct route refuses). There is no keyless
# tier: without a key the Jupiter route refuses with "JUPITER_API_KEY missing".
# Create it at https://developers.jup.ag/portal (a server key with the Swap permission, never a
# "publishable" key).
JUPITER_API_KEY=

# ── Needed only together with the PumpStriker Engine ────────────

# Shared secret of the Engine <-> bot link (the bot answers the Engine's events and
# calls the Engine's /api/bot/v1 and /api/skills routes with it). Manual trading does not need it.
# With the Engine: at least 32 characters, and the SAME value in the Engine's .env.
# Unset or shorter = the link refuses EVERYTHING (fail closed) and the
# bot warns at startup in the log and in Telegram.
# Generate one with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Start order after setting/changing it: restart the Engine FIRST, then the bot.
SIGNAL_SHARED_SECRET=

# ── Safety switch ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1 = dry run FOR AUTO-BUY: it builds and signs, then stops; nothing is sent and the virtual position is
# managed on paper. Your own buys and sells in Telegram and the exits of real positions are NOT affected
# by this switch. Set 0 only after an arm's go-live PASS and your own decision (auto-buy also stays off
# until you arm it with /autobuy). Unset or anything but 0/false/no/off = dry run.
BOT_DRY_RUN=1

# ── Server and Engine link ──────────────────────────────────────

# Port the bot's HTTP events endpoint listens on (127.0.0.1 only). 0 (a random free
# port) is accepted only when NODE_ENV=test.
BOT_PORT=3748

# Engine HTTP API (token / price proxies, skill commands: /dd, /report, ...)
ENGINE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1002

# Folder of wallets.json, settings.json, positions-v2.json, ... (default: ./data).
# The cutover passes the absolute path of the live folder.
# BOT_DATA_DIR=

# Telegram API root. Leave unset; only the end-to-end harness points it at a local fake.
# TG_API_ROOT=https://api.telegram.org

# ── RPC ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# One explicit RPC endpoint. With RPC_FALLBACKS=off the pooled chain reads use only this URL;
# transaction sends, Send SOL, balance, blockhash and priority-fee reads still use the
# Helius / Alchemy / public lanes. For tests.
# RPC_URL=
# RPC_FALLBACKS=on

# Separate Helius key for the trading lane (rate-limit isolation).
# Leave blank to use HELIUS_KEY for everything.
HELIUS_KEY_BOT=

# Override the Helius URLs if you need to (auto-derived from the keys otherwise).
HELIUS_RPC_URL=
HELIUS_SEND_URL=
HELIUS_RPC_URL_BOT=
HELIUS_SEND_URL_BOT=
# HELIUS_KEY_WALLET=
# HELIUS_RPC_URL_WALLET=

# Alchemy key: first failover lane of the bot's RPC pool (optional).
# ALCHEMY_API_KEY=

# Override the Helius Sender URL (defaults to https://sender.helius-rpc.com/fast).
# Helius publishes regional endpoints — use the one closest to your VPS.
# HELIUS_SENDER_URL=

# ── Jupiter and the other venues ────────────────────────────────

# A second Jupiter server key (optional; JUPITER_API_KEY itself is in the REQUIRED block above). It is
# tried only after a 401/403 or 429/5xx of the first one; rate limits are per organisation, so it only
# helps when it belongs to another organisation.
JUPITER_API_KEY_FALLBACK=

# Use Jupiter v3 atomic order+execute endpoint. Currently gated on the
# Jupiter Developer Platform side — leave at 0 (default) unless your
# tier supports it. With 0 the bot uses the legacy /v1/quote + /v2/build
# path which is fully working.
# USE_JUPITER_V3=0

# After Jupiter /swap/v2/build answers "No routes found" (a market Metis dropped or never listed),
# try ONE Jupiter /swap/v2/order + /execute (OKX, Dflow or Metis may still route it; JupiterZ RFQ is
# excluded). off (default) | sell (exits only) | both. The order is refused before signing unless the
# wallet is its fee payer and only signer, its priority fee is at most 2x the setting, and its
# simulation moves only what was asked. /order charges Jupiter's platform fee: 0.5% on tokens under
# 24 hours old, 0.1% on older memecoins.
# JUP_ORDER_FALLBACK=off

# Test overrides (same pattern as TG_API_ROOT): a base URL replaces the host of every
# Jupiter / PumpPortal / pump.fun endpoint of the execution layer. Leave unset in production.
# JUP_API_BASE=https://api.jup.ag
# PUMPPORTAL_BASE=https://pumpportal.fun
# PUMPFUN_API_BASE=https://frontend-api-v3.pump.fun

# ── Helius Backrun Rebates (50% MEV revshare back to YOUR wallet) ──
# Reference: https://www.helius.dev/docs/sending-transactions/backrun-rebates
#
# There is NO default wallet: rebates do nothing until you set a wallet of your own,
# with the /rebates Telegram command (set the wallet, then turn it on) or here, where
# BOTH must be set:
#   1. ENABLE_BACKRUN_REBATES=1   (the default)
#   2. HELIUS_MARKETING_WALLET=<your-own-Solana-base58-pubkey>
#
# /rebates saves its setting in settings.json in the bot data folder (BOT_DATA_DIR),
# the file the send path reads. Once that file has a rebates section, only /rebates
# counts and the two values here are ignored; any setting you save in Telegram writes
# that section (OFF unless you turned rebates on there).
ENABLE_BACKRUN_REBATES=1
HELIUS_MARKETING_WALLET=

# ── Dynamic priority fees via Helius getPriorityFeeEstimate ─────
# Set to 0 to revert to a fixed default (50k microLamports).
ENABLE_DYNAMIC_PRIORITY_FEE=1
PRIORITY_FEE_LEVEL_NORMAL=High
PRIORITY_FEE_LEVEL_SNIPE=VeryHigh
PRIORITY_FEE_CACHE_MS=5000
PRIORITY_FEE_FLOOR_MICROLAMPORTS=50000
PRIORITY_FEE_CEILING_MICROLAMPORTS=5000000

# ── Helius Sender keepalive (cold-start latency) ────────────────
ENABLE_SENDER_KEEPALIVE=1
SENDER_KEEPALIVE_MS=45000

# ── Auto-buy (defaults shown; leave commented to keep them) ─────
# Auto-buy stays off until the owner turns it on in Telegram (mode off / dry_run / live).
# A value that is not valid (or looser than the code default) is logged and the code default is used.
# AUTOBUY_STALE_MS=5000             # signal price older than this at the buy = skip
# AUTO_BUY_MAX_PER_HOUR=3
# AUTO_BUY_MAX_PER_DAY=20
# AUTO_BUY_MAX_SOL_PER_DAY=         # unset = 20 x the buy amount
# AUTO_BUY_MAX_OPEN_POSITIONS=5
# AUTO_BUY_RESERVE_SOL=0.03         # SOL kept back for exit fees; auto-buy skips below buyAmount + reserve
# AUTOBUY_MAX_IMPACT_PCT=15         # placeholder: a route whose own price impact is higher is refused
# AUTOBUY_PAUSE_AFTER_FAILS=3
# AUTOBUY_ROLLING_N=50
# AUTOBUY_ROLLING_MIN_NET=-0.15

# ── Auto-buy PILOT (A-114; off unless AUTOBUY_PILOT is exactly 1/true/on/yes) ─────
# Live auto-buy with tiny amounts BEFORE any arm has a go-live PASS (a fresh Engine reports no arms).
# The pilot relaxes ONLY the go-live PASS. You still type /autobuy on <ARM> (or /autobuy on with no arm
# when the Engine reports no arms: grade A only) and then CONFIRM PILOT <ARM|GRADE-A> in Telegram;
# BOT_DRY_RUN=0 is still needed for a real send; every other guard (staleness, impact cap, reserve,
# hour/day/SOL caps, max open positions, pauses, one buy per token, exits) stays on.
# Never buys a killed, rug-flagged, launch-run (or launch-run unknown), safety-pending or unpriceable token, nor an arm rated
# FAIL/RETIRED/BIASED. A normal PASS confirmation keeps its PASS rule while the pilot is on (its buys get the pilot size and count).
# Buy size = min(buy amount, AUTOBUY_PILOT_MAX_SOL). Pilot buys are counted when sent, per UTC day,
# in data\autobuy-caps.json (a restart does not reset them).
# AUTOBUY_PILOT=0
# AUTOBUY_PILOT_MAX_SOL=0.01        # SOL per pilot buy at most; must be > 0, above 0.05 is clamped to 0.05, invalid = pilot refused
# AUTOBUY_PILOT_MAX_PER_DAY=3       # pilot buys per UTC day; whole number 1..10, above 10 is clamped to 10, invalid = pilot refused

# ── Exits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EXIT_POLICY_ID=EA
# EXIT_YOUNG_CADENCE_MS=5000        # exit pass interval while a position is younger than EXIT_YOUNG_AGE_MS
# EXIT_CADENCE_MS=15000
# EXIT_YOUNG_AGE_MS=600000
# EXIT_QUOTE_CONCURRENCY=4
# HARD_RUG_DROP_PCT=40              # % drop between two checks that triggers a max-slippage rug dump; 0 = off

# ── Telegram delivery ───────────────────────────────────────────
# TG_REPLAY_MAX_AGE_MS=600000       # a signal card older than this (from the decision time) is not sent
# TG_LATE_MS=30000                  # a card older than this carries the LATE banner
# TG_PACE_MAX_PER_10MIN=8           # signal cards per 10 minutes (alerts and ops messages are not paced)

# ── Wallet link routes (the browser-wallet flows of the Engine dashboard) ──
# The dashboard can connect a browser wallet and trade from it. For that the bot only BUILDS an unsigned
# transaction for the connected wallet (that wallet is the only signer and fee payer), reads the chain and
# verifies a fill: it never signs or sends anything for these flows and holds no key of that wallet. The six
# routes under /v1/wallet sit behind the same link auth as /v1/events. The SOL amount, the slippage and the
# price-impact limit are typed per trade in the dashboard; there is no SOL maximum here. Wallet reads use the
# wallet RPC lane (HELIUS_KEY_WALLET, else HELIUS_KEY) behind a global per-second bucket, so wallet work
# cannot take the provider's allowance from auto-buy and the exits.
# WALLET_LINK_ROUTES=1                         # 0 = the six routes are not mounted (they answer 404)
# WALLET_RPC_DAILY_CALL_CAP=3000               # wallet RPC calls per UTC day, all wallet traffic together
# WALLET_RPC_MAX_PER_SEC=4                     # 1-10: calls per second for all wallet traffic together
# WALLET_FILL_POLL_MS=2000                     # fill poll interval in ms, at least 1500
# WALLET_TX_MAX_BYTES=1100                     # largest serialized wallet transaction, at most 1132
# WALLET_PRIORITY_MIN_MICROLAMPORTS=10000      # compute-unit price range of a wallet transaction
# WALLET_PRIORITY_MAX_MICROLAMPORTS=1000000    # at most 1000000

# ── Advanced (rarely needed — leave commented unless you know why) ──

# UnclaimedSol partner key (public attribution key, not a secret). When this is empty the bot
# uses the project's own key, pump-striker: UnclaimedSol then pays the PumpStriker project 20 %
# of UnclaimedSol's own fee on claims made through the menu button.
# Forks: set your own so claims through the menu button credit your partner share.
# UCS_PARTNER_KEY=
